\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write a positive rational number in lowest terms as $r_0=a/b$ with $a,b\in\mathbb N$ and $b$ odd. As long as $r_j>0$, choose the least positive odd integer $d_j$ with $1/d_j\le r_j$ and set $r_{j+1}=r_j-1/d_j$. The choice permits $d_j=1$ and repeated denominators. It is the largest available unit fraction not exceeding the remainder. The expansion terminates if some remainder becomes zero.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does this odd greedy algorithm terminate for every such $a/b$, yielding $a/b=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}1/d_j$ with finitely many terms? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.07280}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must the greedy algorithm restricted to odd denominators eventually express every positive rational with odd denominator as a finite sum of unit fractions?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 9400095 (AMR-093-0095). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Joel Louwsma and Joseph Martino, \emph{Rational numbers with odd greedy expansion of fixed length}, arXiv:2309.07280v1 (2023) Introduction, pp. 1--2. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.07280}.
\item[S3] Wikipedia contributors, \emph{List of unsolved problems in mathematics}, revision 1366636767 (2026) Number theory, Does every rational number with an odd denominator have an odd greedy expansion. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Number_theory}.
